# socket-smoke

A portable application for the board's SD socket below any file system: it
asks mm.sdcard.socket's card for its geometry, prints the block count and
size, then saves the last block, writes a pattern over it, reads the pattern
back, and writes the saved block back, checking each step. It names no pin:
the board's socket provider knows them. On storage-linux-x86_64 and
storage-linux-aarch64 the card is the emulated one in sdcard.img; on a Pico
board it is the card in the TF socket.

The last block is outside every file system mkfs.fat makes on a whole card,
and it is restored, so the card's contents are as they were. An empty socket
skips, exiting 0.

Exit codes:

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | The round trip passed, or the socket was empty and the run skipped. |
| 1 | The card answered its geometry with another failure. |
| 2 | Reading the last block failed. |
| 3 | Writing the pattern failed. |
| 4 | The pattern did not read back. |
| 5 | Restoring the saved block failed; the last block holds the pattern. |
